<p>The Pentagon said Friday the United States will withdraw about 5,000 troops from Germany over the next six to 12 months, reducing the U.S. military presence in a NATO ally that hosts the largest American deployment in Europe. The decision followed President Trump’s threat to pull forces after German Chancellor Friedrich Merz criticized Washington’s conduct of the U.S. war with Iran; Pentagon spokesman Sean Parnell said it followed a review of the U.S. force posture in Europe and reflected “theater requirements and conditions on the ground”. President Trump said Saturday in Florida that the reductions would go beyond the initial Pentagon announcement. German officials played down the immediate impact: Defense Minister Boris Pistorius called the drawdown “foreseeable,” and Berlin said Europe would need to carry more of its own security burden.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/_VkKRpGdAhl5vwXD7qWRyvYSmHSp1IvwMc-WvkfCYVk_Q7TYptmTnsfFJ3dx_H_4qfFlt8y0T2xaCmkl6AvUElU3ol-n3Ebu6DXrd1cVlDsWg-fyLK2xf6g2Swvgm8pjjYKpWnNrSmkr294pbmOThnspaoh0yeTqc7oqzfL_7H_m-fTyxviFhqrqNC7p9RmFy_ZWEBkSY-kyyI0BufjijkbF' alt='Soldiers at the U.S. Army training facility in Hohenfels, Germany.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Remaining force: Politico said the reduction would leave about 33,000 U.S. troops in Germany, while other accounts put the current presence at roughly 35,000 to almost 40,000 personnel.</li><li>Missile plan: The decision also dropped a Biden-era plan to deploy a U.S. battalion with long-range Tomahawk missiles to Germany, according to Dawn and Minute Mirror accounts.</li><li>Possible next withdrawals: NPR reported that Spain and Italy could face similar U.S. troop-withdrawal pressure as allies reassessed reliance on Washington.</li><li>Wider pressure on allies: Minute Mirror, citing Reuters, said Pentagon officials had discussed measures against NATO members viewed as insufficiently supportive of U.S.-led efforts in Iran.</li></ul><blockquote>We’re going to cut way down. And we’re cutting a lot further than 5,000 - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Pentagon: A senior Pentagon official, speaking anonymously, described recent German remarks as “inappropriate and unhelpful” and said the president was reacting to “counterproductive remarks.” (<a href='https://english.aaj.tv/news/330457923/us-withdrawing-5000-troops-from-germany-us-officials-say'>AAJ TV</a>)</li><li>German Defense Ministry: Boris Pistorius said the U.S. presence in Europe, especially in Germany, remained in the interest of both sides of the trans-Atlantic partnership. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/germany-focuses-on-shared-interests-after-us-announces-troop-drawdown/article70934273.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>Republican armed services leaders: Roger Wicker and Mike Rogers objected to moving forces out of Europe and said any posture change should move U.S. troops eastward instead of reducing the forward presence. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/may/02/us-israel-war-iran-germany-american-troops-donald-trump-middle-east-latest-news-updates'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>NATO: A NATO spokesperson said the alliance was seeking details from Washington while maintaining confidence in its ability to provide deterrence and defense as Europe increases its defense role. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3352254/trumps-us-troop-removal-germany-rebuked-republicans-nato'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/nato-top-republicans-question-us-troop-withdrawal-from-germany'>Trump says US will cut its number of troops in Germany ‘way down’</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/4cabedc3-0119-4c3f-9867-094b7e1398f6'>Pentagon to pull 5,000 soldiers out of Germany in dispute over Iran war</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/may/02/